## The Year of Class I Recalls

As of today, there have been 217 Class I recalls reported this year, marking a significant proportion of the total 781 recalls. Class I, for those not familiar, refers to situations where there is a reasonable probability that the use of or exposure to a product will cause serious adverse health consequences or death. The data demands our attention.

## High Offender Brands

Digging into the data, it's evident that certain brands are repeat offenders. Notable culprits include "Spring & Mulberry Inc." and "Moonlight Packing Corporation," each with over 10 Class I recalls. These brands have consistently failed to meet safety standards, earning them a dismal grade of "F."

## Leading Recall Causes

This year has seen 311 recalls due to allergens, topping the list, followed by Salmonella (200 cases) and foreign objects in food (101 cases). Contaminants like Listeria and E. coli have also caused concern, with 116 and 14 cases respectively, illustrating the ongoing struggle to ensure food safety across the board.

## A Look at Recent Events

The most recent recalls, reported on July 15, 2026, include:

- **Adams Extract & Spice LLC** recalled multiple products for undeclared sesame allergens.
- **Fresh & Ready Foods LLC** faced a Class I recall due to raw chicken patties posing Salmonella risks.
- **Trafa Pharmaceutical Inc.** saw Salmonella contamination in its organic moringa leaf powder product.

## Conclusion

With over 1,139 recalls tracked all-time and rising trends in severe cases, it's clear that food safety remains a critical issue. As consumers, vigilance is essential, and as industries, accountability must be enforced.
